Lung Cancer: Lung cancer was the most commonly diagnosed cancer as well as the leading cause of cancer death in Males in 2008 globally. Among females, it was the fourth most commonly diagnosed cancer and the second leading cause of cancer death. Lung cancer accounts for 13% (1.6 million) of the total cases and 18% (1.4 million) of the deaths in 2008. In males, the highest lung cancer incidence rates are in Eastern and Southern Europe, North America, Micronesia and Polynesia and Eastern Asia, while rates are low in sub-Saharan Africa. In females, the highest lung cancer incidence rates are found in North America, Northern Europe and Australia/ New Zealand. Despite their lower prevalence of smoking (less than 4% adult smokers),

